 ## Live traffic (app)
 
 **Extra layers → Traffic** draws the other aeroplanes heard on ADS-B: a top-down aircraft, nose
-along its track, with callsign and flight level beside it, and the full read-out on a tap. A helicopter is drawn as one — rotor, cabin and tail — by its emitter category (rotorcraft) or its type code, and the read-out names it (*R44 · Helicopter*). Aircraft on the ground (or taxiing) are drawn smaller and faded, with their callsign only from airport zoom in; where labels would overprint, one shows and a tap names the rest. When an aircraft sits over a map point — an airfield, VOR, nav waypoint or frequency-change arrow — the tap opens the **Choose point** picker with the aircraft among the choices, so you can read the aeroplane or the point beneath it. The read-out's **Open on adsb.lol** shows the same aircraft on the public map of the site the traffic comes from.
+along its track, with callsign and flight level beside it, and the full read-out on a tap. A helicopter is drawn as one — rotor, cabin and tail — by its emitter category (rotorcraft) or its type code, and the read-out names it (*R44 · Helicopter*). Aircraft on the ground (or taxiing) are drawn smaller and faded, with their callsign only from airport zoom in; where labels would overprint, one shows and a tap names the rest. When an aircraft sits over a map point — an airfield, VOR, nav waypoint, frequency-change arrow, or one of your own route waypoints — the tap opens the **Choose point** picker with the aircraft among the choices, so you can read the aeroplane or the point beneath it. The read-out's **Open on adsb.lol** shows the same aircraft on the public map of the site the traffic comes from.
 It exists in the Android and iPhone/iPad apps only — the public feeds refuse browser requests — so in a
 browser the switch is shown dimmed with the reason. See
 [GPS Track Recording](GPS-Track-Recording#traffic-around-you-ads-b). A cockpit ADS-B receiver
